@meta
id: 09-20
title: Scenario — the refund window
chapter: 09
type: scenario
skills: op-motion, op-counts, op-linewise, op-to-mark
par: 285
minutes: 14
vscode: three tabs, a lot of selecting and retyping
open: order.provider.ts order.entity.ts errors.json

@brief
A ticket, not an exercise.

    ORD-418  Cancelled orders are refunded no matter how old they are.
             Finance want a 30 day window. Past that, cancelling still
             works, but no money moves and the reason is recorded.

Three files, already open as three buffers. `:bn` and `:bp` move between
them; `:ls` lists them.

  1. order.entity.ts — `Order` needs a `placedAt: Date;` field, directly
     under `customerId`. `OrderStatus` is fine as it is.

  2. order.provider.ts — in `cancel`, before the refund call, work out
     whether the order is inside the window:

         const days = (Date.now() - order.placedAt.getTime()) / 86_400_000;

     Refund only when `days <= 30`. When it is outside the window, set
     `cancellationReason: 'REFUND_WINDOW_EXPIRED'` in the update values
     instead of refunding. The order still becomes `CANCELLED` either way.

  3. errors.json — nothing to add. It is open so you can check the codes
     exist, and to prove you can leave a file alone. Change it and the
     check fails.

The dead `legacyRefund` method in the provider goes. It has been unused
since the payment service was rewritten.

@keys
| `d}` | delete to the end of the paragraph |
| `dap` | delete a whole paragraph including its blank line |
| `d/text` `Enter` | delete from here up to the next match of `text` |
| `>i{` | indent everything inside the braces |
| `:bn` `:bp` `:ls` | next buffer, previous buffer, list them |
| `ma` then `d'a` | mark a line, then delete from the cursor to it |

@hints
Do the entity first — it is one `o` and one line, and it tells you what
name to use in the provider.
--
`legacyRefund` is a whole method with a blank line after it and none
inside it, so to vim it is one paragraph. Put the cursor on its first line
and `dap` takes the method and the gap in one go — no counting lines, no
selecting. (A method with a blank line in its body is two paragraphs;
`dap` would take only the first. `d}` twice, or a visual selection, covers
that case.)
--
For the conditional, write the `if` and `else` skeleton first with `o`,
then move the two existing lines into it. `>>` indents a line; `3>>`
indents three. `>i{` indents everything inside the nearest braces, which
is usually what you actually wanted.
--
Your keystroke count will be dominated by typing the new code, and that
is fine. Par assumes you type the new lines once and never select, cut or
retype anything that is already correct.

@solution
A route that measures 248 keystrokes, buffer by buffer.

**order.entity.ts** — `:bn`, then `/customerId` `Enter`, `o`, type
`placedAt: Date;` (autoindent supplies the two spaces), `Esc`, `:w`.

**order.provider.ts** — `:bp` back. Delete the dead method: `/legacyRefund`
`Enter` then `dap`. Find the refund: `/await this.paymentService` `Enter`.
`O` opens a line above it; type the `const days` line, `Enter` twice, then
`if (days <= 30) {` and `Esc`.

Now the part worth stealing. The return block that follows the refund has
to appear twice — once inside the `if`, once after it with the extra
reason. Do not type it twice. `j` `2j` to the `return` line, `4yy` to take
the four lines, `2k` back to `await`, then `6>>` indents the refund, the
gap and the return block into the `if` in one stroke. `5j` to the `});`,
`o` then `Ctrl-D` then `}` to close the `if` one level out. `o` `Esc` for
the gap, `p` to put the copy, `2j` to its `values` line, `f'` `;` to the
closing quote of `'CANCELLED'`, and `a` to append the reason. `Esc`,
`:wqa`.

**errors.json** — do not touch it.

The point of this one is that nothing in it is a new key. Every move is
something chapter 8 and 9 already gave you, and the job still takes three
files and a real decision. That gap — knowing the keys, and reaching for
the right one under mild pressure on unfamiliar code — is the only gap
that matters, and repetition is the only thing that closes it.

Three habits worth stealing from the solution: search to navigate rather
than scrolling to navigate; delete by structure (`dap`) rather than by
counting lines; and when the same block must exist twice, yank it once
and edit the copy. Counting lines is how you end up off by one. Retyping
is how you end up with two versions that differ by a character.

@start order.entity.ts
export type Order = {
  id: string;
  customerId: string;
  status: OrderStatus;
  total: number;
  cancellationReason?: string;
};

export type OrderStatus = 'PENDING' | 'SHIPPED' | 'CANCELLED';

@expected order.entity.ts
export type Order = {
  id: string;
  customerId: string;
  placedAt: Date;
  status: OrderStatus;
  total: number;
  cancellationReason?: string;
};

export type OrderStatus = 'PENDING' | 'SHIPPED' | 'CANCELLED';

@start order.provider.ts
import { injectable, inject } from 'tsyringe';
import { exception } from '../../configuration/errors';
import { OrderRepository } from './order.repository';
import { PaymentService } from '../../infrastructure/services/payment.service';

@injectable()
export class OrderProvider {
  constructor(
    @inject(OrderRepository) protected orderRepository: OrderRepository,
    @inject(PaymentService) protected paymentService: PaymentService,
  ) {}

  public async findOne(id: string) {
    const order = await this.orderRepository.findOne({ where: { id } });

    if (!order) {
      throw exception('ORDER_NOT_FOUND');
    }

    return order;
  }

  public async legacyRefund(id: string) {
    const order = await this.findOne(id);
    return this.paymentService.refundLegacy(order.id, order.total);
  }

  public async cancel(id: string) {
    const order = await this.findOne(id);

    if (order.status === 'SHIPPED') {
      throw exception('ORDER_ALREADY_SHIPPED');
    }

    await this.paymentService.refund(order.id);

    return this.orderRepository.update({
      where: { id },
      values: { status: 'CANCELLED' },
    });
  }
}

@expected order.provider.ts
import { injectable, inject } from 'tsyringe';
import { exception } from '../../configuration/errors';
import { OrderRepository } from './order.repository';
import { PaymentService } from '../../infrastructure/services/payment.service';

@injectable()
export class OrderProvider {
  constructor(
    @inject(OrderRepository) protected orderRepository: OrderRepository,
    @inject(PaymentService) protected paymentService: PaymentService,
  ) {}

  public async findOne(id: string) {
    const order = await this.orderRepository.findOne({ where: { id } });

    if (!order) {
      throw exception('ORDER_NOT_FOUND');
    }

    return order;
  }

  public async cancel(id: string) {
    const order = await this.findOne(id);

    if (order.status === 'SHIPPED') {
      throw exception('ORDER_ALREADY_SHIPPED');
    }

    const days = (Date.now() - order.placedAt.getTime()) / 86_400_000;

    if (days <= 30) {
      await this.paymentService.refund(order.id);

      return this.orderRepository.update({
        where: { id },
        values: { status: 'CANCELLED' },
      });
    }

    return this.orderRepository.update({
      where: { id },
      values: { status: 'CANCELLED', cancellationReason: 'REFUND_WINDOW_EXPIRED' },
    });
  }
}

@start errors.json
{
  "ORDER_NOT_FOUND": { "statusCode": 404, "sentry": false },
  "ORDER_ALREADY_SHIPPED": { "statusCode": 409, "sentry": false },
  "PAYMENT_REFUND_FAILED": { "statusCode": 502, "sentry": true }
}
